    "question": {
      ":question/id": "68bf99a3-7fc9-5547-9120-f2c14067205b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186096652/plots/9c6432dbde91433c0eda3069a5acdb7b90b1dd92fd2c122aa6ace4afc7e20b5a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186096652/plots/ce196804e5d7b784af548a29733c42bab9dcd0e8b224cc7811204d9fb7642530.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=p+q.$\n\nSince $p\u2226q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =1$",
