      ":question/worked-solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186096652/plots/f21af8dde10cb856dfb886ad99c261c5c407a30611019b76e592938efd9e14bf.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{4}{3}|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\frac{4}{3}q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{15}{4}|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=-\\frac{15}{4}p.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}q-\\frac{15}{4}p \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}p+\\frac{4}{3}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-\\frac{15}{4}p+\\frac{4}{3}q.$\n\nSince $p\u2226q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{15}{4}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{4}{3}$",
